#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114974 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115394 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8d3a4ec-7531-5a58-8454-d19869fb21eb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Continuity of a Vector Function"}] :question/id #uuid "f0307723-f211-541e-96c0-e4212c9ecf32" :question/math-academy-id "e-2483" :question/problem "Is the vector function $\\mathbf f(t) = -\\sin t \\,\\mathbf i + (t^2 + 1)\\,\\mathbf j + \\tan t\\,\\mathbf k$ continuous at $t = 0?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the limit of $\\mathbf f (t)$ as $t$ approaches $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}({t}^{2}+1),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,{0}^{2}+1,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =f(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\displaystyle \\lim_{t \\rightarrow t_0} \\mathbf f (t) = \\mathbf f (t_0)$ and therefore $\\mathbf f (t)$ is continuous at $t=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114975 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c68664c1-bc59-51c1-932c-d6f7c8476470" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Vector Function Using Properties of Continuous Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "e08c55e9-d21c-5a33-890c-509847fbbbe8" :question/math-academy-id "e-2484" :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf g(1)$ given that $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $\\mathbf f(1) = \\left\\langle 1, 1, -1 \\right\\rangle$ and \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{t\\rightarrow 1} \\big[ 2\\mathbf f(t)-\\mathbf g(t) \\big] = \\left\\langle 2, 1, -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple rule and the addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)-g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 1}2f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 1}[-g(t)] \\\\  & =2{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)-{lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =2f(1)-g(1) \\\\  & =2{{⟨1,1,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{f(1)}-g(1) \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-2⟩-g(1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{t\\rightarrow 1} \\big[ 2\\mathbf f(t)-\\mathbf g(t) \\big] = \\left\\langle 2, 1, -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $\\mathbf{g} (1)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)-g(t)] & =⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,2,-2⟩-g(1) & =⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ g(1) & =⟨2,2,-2⟩-⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ g(1) & =⟨0,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114976 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115396 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b06f36c-5690-5392-a40a-f670e7419923" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Differentiability of a Vector Function"}] :question/id #uuid "d2650d6c-2bbf-5f1e-bed4-dbba454334cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-2485" :question/problem "Is the vector function $\\mathbf{f}(t) = \\left\\langle 2e^t, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{t-3}, \\: \\sqrt{t} \\right\\rangle$ differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t \\in [0, 2]?$" :question/worked-solution "The function is defined on $t \\in [0, 2].$ Remember that a function is differentiable on the interval $[0, 2]$ if and only if all of its component functions are differentiable on that interval.\n\nLet's now differentiate $\\mathbf f(t)$ to find $\\mathbf f'(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\pi }{t-3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{t},\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{(t-3)^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the third component of this vector, $\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}$ is undefined at $t = 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that the function $\\mathbf f(t)$ is **not** differentiable on $[0,2].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114977 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74401a99-8151-5848-bfcc-1eef2569c204" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Interval on Which a Product of Functions is Differentiable"}] :question/id #uuid "0b25dc9c-70f3-5be6-87d1-cf2a29dd39e9" :question/math-academy-id "e-2486" :question/problem "Find the interval where the function $\\mathbf g(t)\\cdot\\mathbf h(t)$ is differentiable,  given the following functions $\\mathbf g(t)$ and $\\mathbf h(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j-\\sqrt{t}\\,k \\\\ h(t) & =2t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\mathbf F(t) = \\mathbf g(t)\\cdot\\mathbf h(t)$ is differentiable over the common domain where both $\\mathbf g(t)$ and $\\mathbf h(t)$ are differentiable. Remember that a vector function is differentiable if and only if each component is differentiable.\n\nSo, let's start by finding the intervals where each of the functions $\\mathbf g(t)$ and $\\mathbf h(t)$ are differentiable.\n\nFor the function $\\mathbf g(t)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- The first component, $3\\sin t,$ and  the second component, $-\\cos(2t),$ are  differentiable over $(-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n- The third component $- \\sqrt t$ is differentiable on $(0, \\infty).$ Its derivative is not defined at $t=0$ and the function is not defined for $t <0.$\n\nThe intersection of the intervals of differentiability of all three components of $\\mathbf g(t)$ is $(0,\\infty).$  So, $\\mathbf g(t)$ is differentiable on $(0,\\infty).$\n\nFor the function $\\mathbf h(t)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- All three component functions are defined and are differentiable for all values of $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$ \n\nSo, $\\mathbf h(t)$ is differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for $\\mathbf g(t)$ and $\\mathbf h(t)$  is $(0, \\infty) \\cap (-\\infty, \\infty) =  (0,\\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115761 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115394 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8d3a4ec-7531-5a58-8454-d19869fb21eb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Continuity of a Vector Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86734a20-7239-53af-8c14-4b0571e0af66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(t)$ and $h(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113533} {:answer/id #uuid "a1feb6d6-b9c1-5478-afa8-f996c92f15bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the functions are continuous at $t = 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113534} {:answer/id #uuid "db128928-7518-5c5b-8cfe-bf3d91157c50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(t)$ and $h(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113535} {:answer/id #uuid "e1fe392e-e1ac-52ad-b4f5-27498995f854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$h(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113536} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6c02a4-d696-5905-9ea0-e39e7c594871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113537}] :answer-field/id #uuid "02b0b012-b0b7-563a-8f4b-d1624ee864e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113532}] :question/id #uuid "dd56ae52-cc65-50ec-b35f-bed617e7fb7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-32292" :question/problem "Which of the following vector-valued functions are continuous at $t = 0$?\n$f(t)|= \\cot ti + 3t^{3}j + (2t + 1)k; g(t)|= \\frac{2}{t}i + t^{3}j + \\sin tk; h(t)|= 2ti + \\cos tj + t^{4}k$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115396 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b06f36c-5690-5392-a40a-f670e7419923" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Differentiability of a Vector Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "820033b9-1555-51ea-b385-5a8c06b808e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p(t)$ and $r(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113593} {:answer/id #uuid "a572895c-ae65-532f-925a-2a78cfb79c2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the functions are differentiable on $t ∈ (0, 1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113594} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9e5225-8b8f-5ae6-8044-04a37abfb4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q(t)$ and $r(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113595} {:answer/id #uuid "b94bc508-8737-5b62-8438-131d4acb0c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p(t)$ and $q(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113596} {:answer/id #uuid "3f310656-6906-5e49-8065-3e75ec828f1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the functions are differentiable on $t ∈ (0, 1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113597}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5e93cfcc-d1b2-57d7-a7be-139c5f3dd0e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113592}] :question/id #uuid "a626af8f-7439-5291-9587-816c4526871e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32698" :question/problem "Which of the following vector-valued functions are differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t ∈ (0, 1)$?\n$p(t)|= ⟨2t - 1, t^{2} + 4,-e^{t}⟩; q(t)|= ⟨(t + 1)^{4}, \\sqrt[3]{t}, \\ln t⟩; r(t)|= ⟨3t + 2, \\arctan t, t^{2} - 2⟩$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c68664c1-bc59-51c1-932c-d6f7c8476470" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Vector Function Using Properties of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cda57d5-d03a-5dc1-95a4-6d2d92